Background and definitions. A Polish space is a separable, completely metrizable topological space. A Polish presentation of a Polish space X is a pair (d, (x_n)_{n∈ℕ}) where d is a complete metric on X compatible with its topology and (x_n) is a dense sequence in X. The presentation is called:

• computable if the function (i, j) ↦ d(x_i, x_j) is a computable real-valued function (uniformly in i, j);
• left-c.e. if (i, j) ↦ d(x_i, x_j) is uniformly left-computable, i.e., the set {(i, j, q) ∈ ℕ × ℕ × ℚ : q < d(x_i, x_j)} is computably enumerable;
• right-c.e. if (i, j) ↦ d(x_i, x_j) is uniformly right-computable, i.e., the set {(i, j, q) ∈ ℕ × ℕ × ℚ : q > d(x_i, x_j)} is computably enumerable.

A computable Polish presentation (d, (x_n)) of a compact Polish space X is called effectively compact if there is a computable procedure that, given any natural number k, outputs a finite list of indices i_1, …, i_m such that the open balls B(x_{i_j}, 2^{-k}) cover X.

Question. Suppose X is a Polish space admitting a left-c.e. Polish presentation (d, (x_n)) under which X is compact and which is effectively compact in the above sense. Must X also admit a Polish presentation (d', (y_n)) on the same underlying set (up to homeomorphism) such that d' is a computable metric and, in addition, the metric d' is right-c.e.? Equivalently, does every effectively compact left-c.e. Polish space have a computable Polish copy whose metric is simultaneously right-c.e.?

